# One JS heap on the T200

Words: [README.md — Words used](../../README.md#words-used-in-this-project).
This is **copy 1** of the one-heap / generation law (copy 2:
[docs/potential bugs.md](../../docs/potential%20bugs.md) Recurring class 1).

Goal: a **standalone Nexys Video T200** (Artix-7 200T FPGA board) that
`LOAD`/`RUN`s HTML games. After the two 640×480 screens, the chip has ~1 MB
for code + JS objects. That is **one** heap. PACMAN’s ghosts and INVADERS’
aliens must live in the same cells the **GC** (Garbage Collector) uses —
otherwise F9 can look playable while a real `.bin` cannot hold the machine.

A JS object handle is `(generation, index)`: which slot, and which lifetime.
If generation does not match, that object was recycled. Report it. Do not
skip the check so a private copy “still looks alive.”

**Do not** give the opcode unit its own `vvars` / `venv_*` / `vobj_*` /
`name_mem` / `stack` / `intern_var` / `cstack` / `json_mem` while the parent
still has the real tables. Two copies will not fit leftover BRAM, and they
go stale (black PACMAN, stuck rAF, heap skew that looks like four title
bugs). Moving those arrays inside exec to dodge illegal ports is the same
mistake. Silent stack reset is not GC. There is **one decoder** since
2026-08-21 (`exec64`); exec32 is deleted, so inspect the parent +
exec64.

**Do:** one 1-D SRAM. Exec: address in, data next clock. Fast `LOAD_VAR`
(`a1`) still hits that SRAM.

```systemverilog
// BAD — “alive” without checking the handle is still this object
if (vobj_alloc[i] == 2'd1) mark = 1;
// GOOD
if (vobj_alloc[i] == 2'd1 && vobj_gen[i] == handle[43:32]) mark = 1;
```

The gen check stays. Indexing `vobj_alloc[i]` in the unique case is still
illegal SRAM — address `i` this clock, compare `vobj_alloc_rdata` next.
Writes from the parent FSM use `vobj_alloc_wr` (and `varr_len_wr` /
`vvars_wr` / `stack_wr`) — never `vobj_alloc[i] <=` in the 7k-line case.
That poke is the synth hang, not a title shortcut (`never-fake-fpga-sim`).
